00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.430
&gt;&gt; As Aditi mentioned, we are first going
to talk about interviews,

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.830
impromptu speaking in the context of
interviews.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:15.820
Before coming to the GSB, I was at a
company where I did a lot of interviews.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.790
And by the end of it, I often felt like
this.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.880
The person I was talking to had been
rambling, I couldn't get to the point,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.608
I couldn't understand what was it that
they had really added to that situation.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:33.680
So, I wanna offer a format for

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.860
you when you're in an interview that you
can use to help structure your answers and

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.370
help your interviewer understand why you
are so awesome and perfect for the job.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:50.900
And that format is STAR, which stands for
Situation, Task, Action, and Result.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:57.870
So, I'll give you a little demonstration,
starting with situation.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.080
And they key with the situation, is that
you don't wanna give too much detail.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.960
The tendency is for people to get into a
lot of detail, and

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.600
the interviewer kind of gets lost in the
situation itself.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.520
You wanna just offer enough information so

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.800
that they understand the context and the
stakes.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.540
So if I were given the question,

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.430
tell me about a time when you were up
against a really tight deadline.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.890
I might say, okay, I had six weeks to get
our company compliant with

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:30.600
various consumer protections regulations
to meet an investor requirement,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.280
and what was at stake was tens of millions
of dollars.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.410
Very brief and to the point.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.340
Next, the task.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.020
The task can also be brief.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.810
My task was to make sure that we met that
deadline that had been set by investors,

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.940
so we got the money that we needed.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.560
End of story.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.580
And then action.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.338
This is where you wanna spend most of your
time.

00:04:53.338 --> 00:04:56.910
You really wanna help the interviewer
understand what is your thought process,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:59.660
what did you do and why did you do it.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.500
So in this case I started with a project
plan and

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.510
I talked to various people across the
company to try to understand how were they

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.960
going to be impacted by these regulations
and what did we need to change.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.950
I came up with the project plan and

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:19.130
after getting buy in, I followed up really
frequently to make sure we

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.680
were actually on track because we are up
against such a tight deadline.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.080
And finally, the result.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.440
Again, the result can be very short and
sweet.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.460
So the result of this situation was that
we met the deadline, because of my work,

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.370
you wanna make sure that you again give
the interviewer a reason to,

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.360
to believe that you were the one that made
this all happen,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.320
and we got the money that we needed.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:49.030
So hopefully that is a helpful example of
how you can master interviewer,

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:53.160
interviews, and help your interviewer see
why you are so great for the job.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.436
[SOUND].

00:05:58.436 --> 00:06:02.803
&gt;&gt; The other professional setting where
you might be asked to speak impromptu is

00:06:02.803 --> 00:06:04.410
in meetings.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.910
In meetings, your boss might approach you
and say,

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.190
Anna, our sales this quarter has decreased
by 10%, what you think we could do?

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.270
Or David, do you think we should invest in
this new investment opportunity?

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.660
In both of these scenarios,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.530
a good structure that could help you out
of anxiety is PREP.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.380
PREP stands for Point, Reason, Example,
and Point.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.780
You could start your speech with a really
strong point of argument and

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:36.840
then support it with a specific reason.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:41.650
You can then move on to a concrete example
showing why

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.310
your point is the one to follow and agree
on.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.170
And to make sure your audience doesn't
forget what your point is after so

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:54.370
much content in, in-between, you can close
your speech again, as stating the point.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.430
You might wanna ask, why is PREP, a
specific good structure to follow?

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:03.772
The major reason is that,

00:07:03.772 --> 00:07:08.150
it can draw in audiences of a di, of
different backgrounds.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.875
Research have shown that, reasons appeals
to our logical left head,

00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:16.980
while examples and stories appeals to our
emotional right half.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.160
So, say you were in the high-stakes
meeting, or cross-functional meeting,

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.277
where you have people, who both tend to
think, where, where you both have

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:29.920
people who tend to think more logically,
to people from the technology side.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:35.110
At the same time, you also have audience
who are easy to resonate emotionally, for

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.900
example, the people from the sales side.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.580
PREP is such good structure that could
make sure that your speech stays

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:46.030
balanced and you don't unfortunately lose
half of your audience by either

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:51.550
focusing too much on the logical reasons
or purely on examples or stories.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:57.090
Now, let's look at an example of how we
could use PREP effectively.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:01.900
Say, you work in a traditional brick and

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:06.720
mortar retailer and you want to suggest
strengthening your online sale effort.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:14.110
So in a meeting you might wanna, you, you
might wanna use PREP, and start like this.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:20.250
We should gradually move our offline sales
focus and capital to online sales.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.990
The major reason is that during the past
years we've seen

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:28.460
offline sales decreasing while online
sales going uphills.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:33.010
In fact, our major competitor Best Buy,
closed over a thousand of

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:37.680
its stores earlier this year and turned
them into distribution centers for

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.500
online sales which has increased online
sales of 30% for the company.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.900
Therefore, I strongly suggest a shift from
offline sales to online sales.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:53.620
See, PREP is actually very easy to use and

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:58.232
can make sure that your impromptu speech
stays balanced and structured.

00:08:58.232 --> 00:09:03.426
So the next time when your boss approaches
you in the meeting and

00:09:03.426 --> 00:09:08.228
asks you what to do to start the sales
sli, sale slides, or

00:09:08.228 --> 00:09:13.928
whether the company should invest in a new
investment opportunity.

00:09:13.928 --> 00:09:14.934
Don't panic.

00:09:14.934 --> 00:09:18.740
Think of the point, reason, example.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.013
Talk about the in a constant instructor
way, and

00:09:22.013 --> 00:09:24.360
end your speech with the point again.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:30.920
In fact, if you looked at what we, went
through just now, you can easily find that

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:36.410
actually used PREP to structure my own
speech to introduce PREP to you all.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.820
We first talked about the point that PREP
is a good structure to

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:42.770
use in impromptu speaking in meetings.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:45.750
And the major reason think that it can
draw in audiences of

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.540
different backgrounds.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.955
We then move on to example of online
sales,

00:09:49.955 --> 00:09:54.360
offline sales, and I closed my speech
again by stating the point, and

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.559
then encouraging you to use PREP in your
next meetings.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:01.218
So, if you feel good about using PREP,

00:10:01.218 --> 00:10:06.008
why don't start start using it in your
next GSB meetings with our fellows.

00:10:06.008 --> 00:10:12.811
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:17.150
&gt;&gt; Next we're gonna talk about impromptu
speaking in social or personal settings.

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.880
Now we all know this can be particularly
intimidating.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.850
I know when I was asked to give a toast at
my dad's wedding reception without

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.750
knowing I was gonna have to do it, I was
absolutely terrified.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:29.370
But thankfully, just like we saw with the
frameworks they'd show for interviews and

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:33.150
meetings, there are simple structures that
you can have in mind beforehand that will

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:34.900
make the experience much more comfortable
and

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.540
easy for you to give an impromptu speech
in a social setting.

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:41.170
So, before I introduce those structures, I
wanna ask, by show of hands,

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:41.720
who's been to talk?

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:44.900
Right.
Now,

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.900
if you've been to talk you guys probably
know that people often start their

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.150
talk by telling a story.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.980
Do you remember back to Sydney's talk,

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.700
she gave us the story about how she
started playing the bassoon,

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.390
then she went on to talk about what that
illustrated about her and her personality.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.050
And as you might guess there's a reason
that people start their talk by

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.170
telling a story.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:04.515
For those same reasons you should use them
when you have to give an impromptu speech

00:11:04.515 --> 00:11:05.810
in a social or personal setting.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.010
First of all, telling a story engages the
audience.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.750
When you're in a social setting, people
can be talking to each other instead of

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:16.410
listening to you and it's a lot more
engaging to listen to a story, better it

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:20.120
is to listen to a set of facts or figures
that you're, you're regurgitating.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:24.390
And second, it's impromptu and it's a lot
easier for you to try to recount the story

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.770
that you know well than it is to try to
memorize things right beforehand and

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.659
rattle off a list of facts that you might
not have a good handle on.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:35.060
Thankfully, the second part of this
structure is also pretty simple.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:38.030
Just explicitly make the point that the
story illustrated.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.120
It might be as simple as describing what
the story made you think or

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.480
how it made you feel.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:45.810
If you're giving a wedding toast it might
be describing how the story illustrated to

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:47.970
you how good of a match the bride and
groom are.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.230
Or if it's a birthday toast,

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.100
it might be how the story illustrated just
how good of a friend that person is.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.510
Again, the value in this structure is that
it's something that's familiar and

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:59.470
known to you, and it'll be easier for you
to recount convincingly to your audience.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.890
And lastly, just finish strong in whatever
manner's appropriate for

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:04.890
the setting you're in.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:09.020
It might be as simple as raising a glass
and asking everyone to celebrate with you.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:12.040
But don't apologize and don't be afraid to
reiterate your point.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.280
It will just help to drive home your
message, and

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.120
it will make you enjoy the speech that
much more.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.350
So we've all learned in this class to
stand and appreciate our applause,

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.510
if you use this structure, you'll have an
easier, more enjoyable time giving your

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.539
next personal impromptu speech, and you'll
be able to enjoy it more.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:32.230
&gt;&gt; Great.
So we've just heard three techniques for

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.470
how you can be better at impromptu
speaking, and

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.740
sound prepared, even, even if, or sound
polished, even if you haven't prepared.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:43.290
Now we wanna invite you to use this the
next time that you're in one of

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.330
these scenarios.

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:47.300
All of you are gonna be interviewing for
jobs in summer and the fall or

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.990
gonna be doing internships and many of us
are gonna be attending weddings or

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.360
other celebrations over the summer.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:56.487
So there's a lot of opportunities in your
immediate future to take a step back and

00:12:56.487 --> 00:12:58.609
reach for one of these three techniques,

00:12:58.609 --> 00:13:02.027
whichever one speaks the most to your
personal style and use them.

00:13:02.027 --> 00:13:07.829
